          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "A note says hypertension and chronic kidney disease. Does the classification assume a relationship between them, and what changes if it does?",
          "answer": "**Yes, the classification presumes a link.** The coding guidance says hypertension and chronic kidney disease (CKD) are paired \"without being told to.\" The \"with\" convention is the reason. When the alphabetic index links two conditions with \"with,\" the classification reads it as \"associated with\" or \"due to\" and presumes the relationship without requiring the record to state it (coding-hypertension-i10; coding-icd10-cm-code-structure-and-conventions). The presumption applies only to pairings the index actually lists, and it is overridden when the documentation says the two are unrelated (coding-icd10-cm-code-structure-and-conventions).\n\n**If the link is presumed** (the note lists both and says nothing about cause), hypertension with CKD moves to combination code I12, with a second code for the stage of kidney disease (coding-hypertension-i10). The hypertension and kidney disease are captured together in one code rather than two separate ones.\n\n**If the documentation states they are unrelated,** the presumption is overridden. The guidance does not spell out in the passages I retrieved which codes apply in that case, so I can't tell you the exact replacement codes from this source. A coder would need the full coding guidelines for that.\n\n**Heart disease works differently.** The classification makes no such presumption for heart disease. Unless the record says the heart condition is hypertensive or due to hypertension, the two are coded separately and I11 is not used (coding-hypertension-i10).\n\nCoding decisions are made by clinicians and coders from the full record. If you have a specific note, the question of how it was coded is worth raising with the clinician or the billing office.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
